{"name":"Deep Bamboo Yellow","hex":"#d99f50","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extending, reaching or positioned far from a point of reference, especially downwards.\nExtending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively.","Extending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Far, especially far down through something or into something, physically or figuratively.","In a profound, not superficial, manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep"},{"term":"bamboo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the fast-growing grasses of the Bambusoideae subfamily, characterized by its woody, hollow, round, straight, jointed stem.","Wood of the bamboo plant as a material for building, furniture, etc."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of the wood of the bamboo."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bamboo"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bamboo","blocks":[{"text":"Bamboos are a diverse group of mostly evergreen perennial flowering plants making up the subfamily Bambusoideae of the grass family Poaceae. Giant bamboos are the largest members of the grass family, in the case of Dendrocalamus sinicus having individual stalks (culms) reaching a length of 46 meters (151 ft), up to 36 centimeters (14 in) in diameter and a weight of up to 450 kilograms (1,000 lb). It grows in tropical and subtropical regions, especially in Asia. The internodes of bamboos can also be of great length. Kinabaluchloa wrayi has internodes up to 2.5 meters (8 ft) in length, and Arthrostylidium schomburgkii has internodes up to 5 meters (16 ft) in length, exceeded in length only by papyrus. By contrast, the stalks of the tiny bamboo Raddiella vanessiae of the savannas of French Guiana measure only 10–20 millimeters (0.4–0.8 in) in length by about 2 millimeters (0.08 in) in width. The origin of the word \"bamboo\" is uncertain, but it most likely comes from the Dutch or Portuguese language, which originally borrowed it from Malay."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Bamboo_forest.jpg/330px-Bamboo_forest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamboo"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"}]